00:29:50.840 --> 00:29:53.470
- The pacing with which we
were able to just jump in

00:29:53.470 --> 00:29:57.750
and get involved, kind of made
it, it made it a no brainer.

00:29:57.750 --> 00:30:00.663
And it also gave me
the opportunity to say,

00:30:01.837 --> 00:30:02.670
"You know what?

00:30:02.670 --> 00:30:05.330
I can play a couple of
different roles here

00:30:05.330 --> 00:30:06.840
and see where I fit in."

00:30:06.840 --> 00:30:09.670
So you'll see where I fit
in as the season unfolds,

00:30:09.670 --> 00:30:10.610
you just wait.

00:30:10.610 --> 00:30:13.420
- So super immersive world

00:30:13.420 --> 00:30:17.000
and the fact that all this can
happen just between your ears

00:30:17.000 --> 00:30:20.320
and it's as big as your
imagination wants to make it.

00:30:20.320 --> 00:30:22.510
It's just something that, it's a medium

00:30:22.510 --> 00:30:23.860
we're excited to be involved with.

00:30:23.860 --> 00:30:27.400
Ronstadt. First two
episodes drop tomorrow.

00:30:27.400 --> 00:30:29.730
Wherever podcasts are found.

00:30:29.730 --> 00:30:34.730
- Okay, before we get into
creative failure and rejection,

00:30:35.290 --> 00:30:36.580
I just wanted to start off with like

00:30:36.580 --> 00:30:38.121
some relational rejection.

00:30:38.121 --> 00:30:43.121
Have you ever, Rhett been
relationally rejected?

00:30:43.470 --> 00:30:44.567
- Of course not.

00:30:44.567 --> 00:30:46.085
(Link laughing)

00:30:46.085 --> 00:30:50.973
- I know you'd set your sights on a girl,

00:30:51.850 --> 00:30:54.150
but that didn't necessarily mean

00:30:54.150 --> 00:30:56.020
that their sights were set on you.

00:30:56.020 --> 00:30:59.260
- You know, I've actually, I've
thought about this recently.

00:30:59.260 --> 00:31:00.850
I actually had a tendency,

00:31:00.850 --> 00:31:03.520
well, I think there's two things going on.

00:31:03.520 --> 00:31:06.064
First of all, I just really like women

00:31:06.064 --> 00:31:08.760
(laughing)

00:31:08.760 --> 00:31:11.170
and that started very early

00:31:11.170 --> 00:31:13.650
and it was very intense,

00:31:13.650 --> 00:31:15.230
like from well, I'm not talking,

00:31:15.230 --> 00:31:16.990
this was like not at puberty.

00:31:16.990 --> 00:31:18.110
This was before puberty.

00:31:18.110 --> 00:31:19.830
This was like first grade. Right?

00:31:19.830 --> 00:31:23.960
I just I had infatuation issues

00:31:23.960 --> 00:31:27.010
and I would kind of get focused on a girl.

00:31:27.010 --> 00:31:28.920
And typically

00:31:30.580 --> 00:31:33.400
I would focus on someone who-

00:31:33.400 --> 00:31:35.880
- You dream about flying to her house.

00:31:35.880 --> 00:31:38.100
- Well, I would focus on girls

00:31:38.100 --> 00:31:41.710
that were for lack of a better
word, unattainable for me.

00:31:41.710 --> 00:31:43.760
Right? Like I don't know what it was.

00:31:43.760 --> 00:31:44.593
It would be like, okay,

00:31:44.593 --> 00:31:46.770
this girl is two years older than me.

00:31:46.770 --> 00:31:47.663
- Mm-hmm.

00:31:48.597 --> 00:31:51.170
- And it would be embarrassing
for her to date me.

00:31:51.170 --> 00:31:52.970
Even if we were in the same grade,

00:31:52.970 --> 00:31:55.230
she probably wouldn't date me, right?

00:31:55.230 --> 00:31:57.419
I mean, you've seen
pictures of me growing up.

00:31:57.419 --> 00:32:00.410
And so I was a little bit gangly

00:32:00.410 --> 00:32:03.213
but I had a lot of confidence and I would,

00:32:04.660 --> 00:32:06.170
and once I kind of made that decision,

00:32:06.170 --> 00:32:11.040
I would begin what I guess
my was my version of flirting

00:32:11.040 --> 00:32:14.670
and talking and telling other
people that I like this girl,

00:32:14.670 --> 00:32:16.050
telling her friends that I like them.

00:32:16.050 --> 00:32:18.117
- Moving and shaking, trying
to get something going.

00:32:18.117 --> 00:32:19.610
- And there were a couple of times

00:32:19.610 --> 00:32:21.220
where this was a successful strategy

00:32:21.220 --> 00:32:23.820
and that was all I needed
to continue doing it.

00:32:23.820 --> 00:32:27.520
But I would say that just
the percentage breakdown,

00:32:27.520 --> 00:32:31.463
it's probably 80, 20, 80%
rejection, 20% success.

00:32:33.380 --> 00:32:36.740
So rejected more often
than I was accepted.

00:32:36.740 --> 00:32:41.740
- So, I didn't think it
was that much of a spread.

00:32:42.070 --> 00:32:44.510
Sorry to use the term.
- I mean...

00:32:44.510 --> 00:32:47.963
- So does that mean that no
one rejection stands out?

00:32:52.140 --> 00:32:55.090
- Well, I mean, if you're talking about

00:32:55.090 --> 00:32:56.980
my biggest crush in high school,

00:32:56.980 --> 00:32:57.813
I don't even consider that a rejection

00:32:57.813 --> 00:33:00.260
because it was so unrealistic.

00:33:00.260 --> 00:33:02.370
But like, there were, I remember a girl-

00:33:02.370 --> 00:33:05.810
- But like do you like, one
where you were like to her face

00:33:05.810 --> 00:33:08.260
saying, "Will you be my girlfriend?"

00:33:08.260 --> 00:33:11.740
Or "Will you go with me" or
"Will you go out on a date?"

00:33:11.740 --> 00:33:13.840
And she was like, "Ah, no.

00:33:13.840 --> 00:33:15.910
- I don't think, well, you know it was

00:33:15.910 --> 00:33:17.020
in like middle school, high school.

00:33:17.020 --> 00:33:21.050
It never really got to the point
where you were face to face

00:33:21.050 --> 00:33:23.060
asking and being rejected.

00:33:23.060 --> 00:33:26.940
The rejection usually
came through a friend

00:33:26.940 --> 00:33:29.800
before you got there.
- That helps. Right.

00:33:29.800 --> 00:33:31.300
- Yeah and I mean, the funny thing is

00:33:31.300 --> 00:33:32.923
is I don't ever remember,

00:33:35.280 --> 00:33:38.020
again, it's hard to
remember exactly emotionally

00:33:38.020 --> 00:33:40.273
what was going on in high school.

00:33:42.500 --> 00:33:45.323
But I would kind of just
be like, okay. All right.

00:33:47.100 --> 00:33:48.233
That's not happening.

00:33:49.100 --> 00:33:50.980
So I would just kind of move on.

00:33:50.980 --> 00:33:53.400
- So you never, like, it never shook you

00:33:54.260 --> 00:33:55.423
that you can recall?

00:33:57.640 --> 00:33:59.140
- No.

00:33:59.140 --> 00:34:00.290
- Hmm.Okay.

00:34:00.290 --> 00:34:04.580
Because for me, I mean, my
problem was the opposite.

00:34:04.580 --> 00:34:09.020
Like I never got, I was
so anxious and self-aware

00:34:09.020 --> 00:34:13.220
and just kind of felt like
I didn't know what I wanted.

00:34:13.220 --> 00:34:16.620
And it was so much about
what should I be doing

00:34:16.620 --> 00:34:19.340
so that I can, you know,
fit in or whatever.

00:34:19.340 --> 00:34:21.900
So like girls would, I would
hear through the grapevine

00:34:21.900 --> 00:34:23.550
that some girl wanted to date me.

00:34:23.550 --> 00:34:26.950
And then I was like, "Oh
crap, this is a huge test.

00:34:26.950 --> 00:34:28.120
I feel like I'm gonna fail."

00:34:28.120 --> 00:34:33.120
There was a lot of fear and
anxiety that short circuited,

00:34:33.980 --> 00:34:36.890
it was just like the polar
opposite of you at that time.

00:34:36.890 --> 00:34:40.330
You know, it short-circuited
so many of these experiences.

00:34:40.330 --> 00:34:42.350
So for me to say, well, you know what?

00:34:42.350 --> 00:34:44.440
I don't think I was ever rejected

00:34:44.440 --> 00:34:46.883
is definitely not a brag at all,

00:34:48.090 --> 00:34:50.180
and I think this is something
that when we talk about

00:34:50.180 --> 00:34:54.381
the creative ramifications
of rejection and failure

00:34:54.381 --> 00:34:59.381
it's this dynamic of fear kind
of keeping you from action.

00:35:00.210 --> 00:35:04.380
And if you overcome that
whether that's to create

00:35:04.380 --> 00:35:07.610
or initiate with somebody
or try to initiate

00:35:07.610 --> 00:35:09.073
some sort of a relationship,

00:35:13.214 --> 00:35:16.310
you never know what can
happen if you go for it

00:35:16.310 --> 00:35:19.230
but you know what's gonna
happen if you don't, nothing.

00:35:19.230 --> 00:35:20.260
- Right.

00:35:20.260 --> 00:35:24.510
- And, I think for me,
the only rejection was,

00:35:24.510 --> 00:35:28.133
I mean, definitely I was dumped by Jana.

00:35:29.050 --> 00:35:30.440
- Yeah.
- My sophomore year.

00:35:30.440 --> 00:35:31.860
She was a freshmen.

00:35:31.860 --> 00:35:33.673
And she wrote me a note
and I put it in my, yeah.

00:35:35.784 --> 00:35:37.380
She wrote me the note
that we were breaking up

00:35:37.380 --> 00:35:38.920
because I was just a friend to her

00:35:38.920 --> 00:35:42.110
because I clammed up so much around her.

00:35:42.110 --> 00:35:46.200
And I was so anxious and in my own head.

00:35:46.200 --> 00:35:48.800
And it was about what
should I be doing and not

00:35:50.234 --> 00:35:51.889
what do I want to do?

00:35:51.889 --> 00:35:56.020
I was so in my own head
and she was very nice

00:35:56.020 --> 00:35:58.520
and she let me off the hook, but it was a,

00:35:58.520 --> 00:36:02.873
I mean, I remember reading
that letter and boy,

00:36:03.820 --> 00:36:05.749
it was embarrassing to be like, okay,

00:36:05.749 --> 00:36:08.083
you're a great person.

00:36:09.000 --> 00:36:11.780
I read the letter on
Good Mythical Morning,

00:36:11.780 --> 00:36:15.370
because once we moved to LA, I found my,

00:36:15.370 --> 00:36:17.187
I brought my soccer bag
out here for some reason,

00:36:17.187 --> 00:36:18.781
- Yeah, got to keep your soccer bag.

00:36:18.781 --> 00:36:21.200
- I thought I was gonna play some soccer

00:36:21.200 --> 00:36:23.440
after not playing for like
a decade or something.

00:36:23.440 --> 00:36:25.413
It was sentimental value to have my cleats

00:36:25.413 --> 00:36:27.230
and stuff in there.

00:36:27.230 --> 00:36:30.163
But I found that I had
kept that rejection letter.

00:36:31.150 --> 00:36:33.510
I guess, I don't know why I did it.

00:36:33.510 --> 00:36:36.600
I'd like to think it
was some sort of lesson

00:36:36.600 --> 00:36:40.040
that then I was gonna
go after what I wanted

00:36:40.040 --> 00:36:45.040
from then forward and I wasn't
gonna let fear clam me up,

00:36:46.110 --> 00:36:49.030
but I don't think that
was actually the case.

00:36:49.030 --> 00:36:53.690
I just think it was just
like, man, this hurts.

00:36:53.690 --> 00:36:56.130
I'm so sentimental.

00:36:56.130 --> 00:36:59.400
I'm gonna shove it in
this bag and I'm never,

00:36:59.400 --> 00:37:01.840
maybe I didn't go in that pocket anymore

00:37:01.840 --> 00:37:02.673
and maybe it was just sentimental.

00:37:02.673 --> 00:37:05.310
- I was giving you good
advice at the time, man.

00:37:05.310 --> 00:37:07.080
I was like this is quite an opportunity

00:37:07.080 --> 00:37:08.430
that you've been given.

00:37:08.430 --> 00:37:11.250
Because she like invited you to her house.

00:37:11.250 --> 00:37:12.083
- I went to her house.

00:37:12.083 --> 00:37:12.916
- And I was like, "Well,

00:37:12.916 --> 00:37:14.300
you should definitely
accept that invitation."

00:37:14.300 --> 00:37:15.670
- Yeah, one of my biggest regrets

00:37:15.670 --> 00:37:17.690
is not riding the four-wheeler with her.

00:37:17.690 --> 00:37:18.801
- Right, yeah, and that's not a euphemism,

00:37:18.801 --> 00:37:20.850
that's literally riding a four-wheeler.

00:37:20.850 --> 00:37:22.090
We were in North Carolina.

00:37:22.090 --> 00:37:25.580
- But I mean, it would
have been so awesome.

00:37:25.580 --> 00:37:30.580
I mean, here I am, you know,
about to turn 43 years old

00:37:31.230 --> 00:37:35.750
and I'm still regretting
not straddling her

00:37:35.750 --> 00:37:37.200
on that four-wheeler.
- Right, yeah.

00:37:37.200 --> 00:37:38.720
- I mean, we both be facing the same,

00:37:38.720 --> 00:37:40.580
I don't know, who knows what directions

00:37:40.580 --> 00:37:42.007
we would have been facing.
- There's lots of things.

00:37:42.007 --> 00:37:42.988
The seat is very, very wide.
- We could have been

00:37:42.988 --> 00:37:44.740
facing every direction, man.

00:37:44.740 --> 00:37:46.230
- The seat is very wide and long.

00:37:46.230 --> 00:37:47.770
- I mean, once I got away from her-

00:37:47.770 --> 00:37:50.260
- There's probably a whole like section

00:37:50.260 --> 00:37:53.633
on some porn website of just
stuff that happens on ATVs.

00:37:53.633 --> 00:37:55.130
I'm not saying I know about it.

00:37:55.130 --> 00:37:57.490
I'm just saying that that's
how specialized things can get.

00:37:57.490 --> 00:38:00.550
- Now, you gotta wield the
power that we have with caution,

00:38:00.550 --> 00:38:01.740
because just saying that

00:38:01.740 --> 00:38:03.487
is gonna like spike that search.

00:38:03.487 --> 00:38:07.480
- What if it generates a
whole new category of porn?

00:38:07.480 --> 00:38:09.440
I mean, we can take credit for that too.

00:38:09.440 --> 00:38:13.275
The Miyagi school of driving,
fresh breath and ATV porn

00:38:13.275 --> 00:38:15.860
(both laughing)

00:38:15.860 --> 00:38:19.610
- Okay, so we're teaching people
how to have sex on an ATV.

00:38:19.610 --> 00:38:20.443
- There's a bunch of ATVs there.

00:38:20.443 --> 00:38:21.590
Are those for driving?

00:38:21.590 --> 00:38:22.536
No, that's just for sex.

00:38:22.536 --> 00:38:25.133
(both laughing)

00:38:25.133 --> 00:38:26.545
- Oh, man.

00:38:26.545 --> 00:38:28.610
- Okay.
- That rejection hurt.

00:38:28.610 --> 00:38:30.170
So I guess, I mean,

00:38:30.170 --> 00:38:32.810
that's all we have to go
into the relational stuff,

00:38:32.810 --> 00:38:36.950
but just get back to the creative stuff,

00:38:36.950 --> 00:38:38.100
let's get in our minds

00:38:38.100 --> 00:38:40.570
what we feel like is not
rejection, but failure.

00:38:40.570 --> 00:38:43.360
What's our like biggest creative failure?

00:38:43.360 --> 00:38:44.650
Do you have something in your mind?

00:38:44.650 --> 00:38:45.850
I have something in my mind.

00:38:45.850 --> 00:38:48.720
- I mean, the first thing
that comes to mind is GMM 22.

00:38:48.720 --> 00:38:50.390
- Yeah, that's my answer.

00:38:50.390 --> 00:38:53.230
And we've, I mean, we devoted
a whole episode at the time.

00:38:53.230 --> 00:38:55.570
It would be interesting to
go back and listen to that.

00:38:55.570 --> 00:38:56.403
- Oh, I'm not gonna go
back and listen to that,

00:38:56.403 --> 00:38:58.130
- I don't want to do that.

00:38:58.130 --> 00:38:59.960
You can do that, because it was like-

00:38:59.960 --> 00:39:02.450
- That was one of my, I am not proud of,

00:39:02.450 --> 00:39:03.593
I'm not, but we can talk about that

00:39:03.593 --> 00:39:05.840
because I think that's a good example

00:39:05.840 --> 00:39:09.680
of the way that failure and rejection

00:39:09.680 --> 00:39:11.060
can kind of get into your head, you know,

00:39:11.060 --> 00:39:13.080
because I got very, well,

00:39:13.080 --> 00:39:15.710
just a little background.
- Categorize what it was.

00:39:15.710 --> 00:39:18.802
- So what year was that?

00:39:18.802 --> 00:39:21.830
2017?

00:39:21.830 --> 00:39:23.940
- I don't know.
- 2018? 2018.

00:39:23.940 --> 00:39:26.150
- 17 and 18 she says.
- 17 and 18. Okay.

00:39:26.150 --> 00:39:28.170
So we were given the opportunity,

00:39:28.170 --> 00:39:29.970
we were approached by YouTube.

00:39:29.970 --> 00:39:32.040
They were investing in
different kinds of content.

00:39:32.040 --> 00:39:33.927
They actually originally came to us,

00:39:33.927 --> 00:39:36.390
I don't know if we've ever
talked about this part of it,

00:39:36.390 --> 00:39:37.450
but they originally came to us

00:39:37.450 --> 00:39:39.150
and asked us if we would make like

00:39:41.536 --> 00:39:43.890
a late show version of
Good Mythical Morning.

00:39:43.890 --> 00:39:46.420
So like a Good Mythical
Night or something like that.

00:39:46.420 --> 00:39:49.180
And we were like, that
sounds like a great idea

00:39:49.180 --> 00:39:53.660
but like "We're already kind of really

00:39:53.660 --> 00:39:55.967
doing all we can with
Good Mythical Morning."

00:39:55.967 --> 00:39:58.774
And we're like, "Is
there some way to expand

00:39:58.774 --> 00:40:01.090
Good Mythical Morning itself?"

00:40:01.090 --> 00:40:02.870
Which going back in time, it kind of feels

00:40:02.870 --> 00:40:05.690
like we should have just
done the late show version.

00:40:05.690 --> 00:40:07.600
But anyway, in addition to GMM,

00:40:07.600 --> 00:40:12.170
because we ended up doing
five parts of a show

00:40:12.170 --> 00:40:13.003
every single day.

00:40:13.003 --> 00:40:14.370
I think it went down to four, but anyway,

00:40:14.370 --> 00:40:16.875
it was like a multi-part thing and-

00:40:16.875 --> 00:40:19.260
- The technology worked against us,

00:40:19.260 --> 00:40:20.503
but also, you know,

00:40:21.490 --> 00:40:24.710
we said, yes, we wanted to go for it.

00:40:24.710 --> 00:40:26.830
And it did not work.

00:40:26.830 --> 00:40:28.760
I mean, long story short, it didn't work.

00:40:28.760 --> 00:40:31.340
And it hurts when it's,

00:40:31.340 --> 00:40:33.440
you know, if you create something

00:40:33.440 --> 00:40:35.860
and you put your heart and soul into it,

00:40:35.860 --> 00:40:37.690
I'm saying this by contrast,

00:40:37.690 --> 00:40:39.230
I'm not describing what we did with that.

00:40:39.230 --> 00:40:43.570
But as an artist if you create something

00:40:43.570 --> 00:40:44.950
that you pour your heart and soul into,

00:40:44.950 --> 00:40:48.440
and then nobody sees it or cares about it,

00:40:48.440 --> 00:40:49.559
that's a certain type of failure.

00:40:49.559 --> 00:40:50.400
- It's a different thing.

00:40:50.400 --> 00:40:52.380
- What we experienced was something

00:40:52.380 --> 00:40:55.740
that was near and dear to
so many Mythical Beasts

00:40:56.720 --> 00:40:58.230
that they cared about so much.

00:40:58.230 --> 00:41:03.230
And we messed with it in
a way that frustrated them

00:41:03.400 --> 00:41:04.840
and was not a good experience

00:41:04.840 --> 00:41:08.775
which then made it an even more
difficult experience for us,

00:41:08.775 --> 00:41:12.340
because we were trying
to take into account

00:41:13.840 --> 00:41:15.905
all of the feed, there
were so much feedback.

00:41:15.905 --> 00:41:16.738
- Yeah.

00:41:16.738 --> 00:41:21.738
- So that was a certain type
of very scrutinized failure.

00:41:21.870 --> 00:41:25.100
It's kind of like the "Waterworld,"

00:41:25.100 --> 00:41:27.500
like big budget movie scenario

00:41:27.500 --> 00:41:30.110
where everybody's geared up to see it

00:41:30.110 --> 00:41:32.280
and then it's just horrible.

00:41:32.280 --> 00:41:36.590
So it becomes the object lesson of

00:41:37.550 --> 00:41:38.943
cinema no-nos.

00:41:40.278 --> 00:41:42.517
And because there was,

00:41:43.440 --> 00:41:47.370
the digital space is
much more experimental

00:41:47.370 --> 00:41:50.023
and frontier-like,

00:41:51.070 --> 00:41:52.120
I don't think it serves,

00:41:52.120 --> 00:41:54.470
and no one's emulating GMM to the point

00:41:54.470 --> 00:41:56.200
where it becomes a cautionary tale.

00:41:56.200 --> 00:42:00.990
So I don't know exactly what we learned,

00:42:00.990 --> 00:42:03.753
but I think I'm sure we
learned a lot of stuff.

00:42:03.753 --> 00:42:06.300
- Well and we both agree that

00:42:07.300 --> 00:42:08.993
we're very glad that we
said yes and we tried it.

00:42:08.993 --> 00:42:11.470
I mean, 'cause there are number of things

00:42:11.470 --> 00:42:12.563
that came from that.

00:42:13.432 --> 00:42:15.950
It honestly, it transformed our business.

00:42:15.950 --> 00:42:16.783
Right?
- Right.

00:42:16.783 --> 00:42:19.944
- We ended up moving
into the space next door,

00:42:19.944 --> 00:42:23.945
and we ended up more
than doubling our staff.

00:42:23.945 --> 00:42:27.290
- The funding that we had,
we put to work in ways

00:42:27.290 --> 00:42:30.414
that we're still seeing
those benefits now.

00:42:30.414 --> 00:42:32.880
- We were able to take so many
of the things that we learned

00:42:32.880 --> 00:42:35.330
just from a creative and
a production standpoint,

00:42:35.330 --> 00:42:37.570
and then incorporate it back into GMM

00:42:37.570 --> 00:42:41.395
when we went back to the
one episode a day version.

00:42:41.395 --> 00:42:43.050
- So, I totally take back what I said.

00:42:43.050 --> 00:42:44.680
It's like I don't know
if we learned anything.

00:42:44.680 --> 00:42:45.840
- No.
- Because I think the point,

00:42:45.840 --> 00:42:48.580
the principle is absolutely we learned

00:42:48.580 --> 00:42:51.066
a whole bunch of stuff and
that's kind of the point

00:42:51.066 --> 00:42:53.943
and it kind of redeems
the experience, right?

00:42:55.643 --> 00:42:56.760
If that didn't work

00:42:56.760 --> 00:43:01.080
but it changed the trajectory
of where we're headed

00:43:01.080 --> 00:43:06.080
and I can't even articulate
all the lessons that we learned

00:43:06.450 --> 00:43:08.700
but they're ingrained in our experience

00:43:08.700 --> 00:43:11.360
and impact the decisions
that we have been making

00:43:11.360 --> 00:43:12.870
ever since then.

00:43:12.870 --> 00:43:17.430
And I think that's a principle of failure

00:43:17.430 --> 00:43:21.840
is it's less of a failure
if you went for something,

00:43:21.840 --> 00:43:24.010
tried something and learned from it,

00:43:24.010 --> 00:43:27.540
than not going for anything
and learning nothing

00:43:27.540 --> 00:43:31.670
and you might've saved face a little bit,

00:43:31.670 --> 00:43:35.410
but I mean, if it would have
destroyed Good Mythical Morning

00:43:35.410 --> 00:43:36.691
and it wouldn't have recovered.

00:43:36.691 --> 00:43:37.524
- Then we would maybe

00:43:37.524 --> 00:43:38.357
be thinking differently.
- I mean, that could happen,

00:43:38.357 --> 00:43:40.070
businesses go bankrupt,

00:43:40.070 --> 00:43:42.330
people try things, they have bad ideas.

00:43:42.330 --> 00:43:44.400
Or they have good ideas that just,

00:43:44.400 --> 00:43:46.910
things out of their control tank it.

00:43:46.910 --> 00:43:49.775
That absolutely could have happened for us

00:43:49.775 --> 00:43:52.590
and then it would have been a regret

00:43:52.590 --> 00:43:55.360
and it would have been
what we call a mistake-

00:43:55.360 --> 00:43:57.089
- But even then-
- But a creative failure-

00:43:57.089 --> 00:44:00.080
- I choose to believe that even then

00:44:00.080 --> 00:44:01.800
it would have led to something else

00:44:01.800 --> 00:44:04.030
because that's just my experience

00:44:04.030 --> 00:44:06.580
is that always there's a lesson.

00:44:06.580 --> 00:44:09.860
Thankfully the lesson didn't
come along with a complete

00:44:11.010 --> 00:44:13.200
fumbling of Good Mythical
Morning and killing it.

00:44:13.200 --> 00:44:14.223
It came back.

00:44:15.250 --> 00:44:16.650
But there's always a lesson and you know,

00:44:16.650 --> 00:44:18.710
and I'm especially grateful for it

00:44:18.710 --> 00:44:21.313
because that's why I
started going to therapy.

00:44:22.490 --> 00:44:25.500
And because of just how
difficult that time was,

00:44:25.500 --> 00:44:28.630
not just the rejection, the
rejection was difficult,

00:44:28.630 --> 00:44:31.570
but it was more just, it
was too much to take on.

00:44:31.570 --> 00:44:34.270
But you know, the reason I
don't want to go back and listen

00:44:34.270 --> 00:44:36.050
to that episode

00:44:38.020 --> 00:44:41.980
where we like essentially
complained and defended ourselves,

00:44:41.980 --> 00:44:45.830
and I did it more than you
did so I take credit for that.

00:44:45.830 --> 00:44:47.550
I was defensive.

00:44:47.550 --> 00:44:49.380
I was in a bad place.

00:44:49.380 --> 00:44:50.213
I was hurt.

00:44:50.213 --> 00:44:53.250
I was trying to, I said
some things I regret.

00:44:53.250 --> 00:44:54.870
Now,

00:44:54.870 --> 00:44:58.730
I think that my, if that
were to happen today,

00:44:58.730 --> 00:45:01.850
post-therapy, my processing
of what was going on

00:45:01.850 --> 00:45:02.683
would be very different.

00:45:02.683 --> 00:45:05.320
Because one of the things,
and is it next week

00:45:05.320 --> 00:45:07.640
that we're gonna start
talking about the Enneagram?

00:45:07.640 --> 00:45:10.040
- Yes. We're gonna do it.

00:45:10.040 --> 00:45:13.060
- So we are gonna start,
so stay tuned next week

00:45:13.060 --> 00:45:14.740
and maybe even the following week,

00:45:14.740 --> 00:45:16.800
we may do a two-part
thing with the Enneagram

00:45:16.800 --> 00:45:18.590
where we're gonna talk
about what the Enneagram is

00:45:18.590 --> 00:45:19.840
a little bit, but more just talk about

00:45:19.840 --> 00:45:21.250
our experience with
it, what numbers we are

00:45:21.250 --> 00:45:23.050
and how that has impacted our lives.

00:45:24.070 --> 00:45:26.290
But I am

00:45:28.150 --> 00:45:30.300
Enneagram three, I'm a performer.

00:45:30.300 --> 00:45:33.690
And there's a number of things
that might go into that,

00:45:33.690 --> 00:45:34.523
meaning that,

00:45:35.670 --> 00:45:37.450
it's part nature, part nurture

00:45:38.580 --> 00:45:42.660
that I have been a very
performance minded person.

00:45:42.660 --> 00:45:46.030
And when you're a person
who finds their worth

00:45:46.030 --> 00:45:48.313
in their success,

00:45:49.970 --> 00:45:53.673
you might also be a person
who finds their worthlessness

00:45:53.673 --> 00:45:56.480
in their failure.
- Their failure.

00:45:56.480 --> 00:45:59.330
- And so I think that there is and again,

00:45:59.330 --> 00:46:00.970
and I have been,

00:46:00.970 --> 00:46:02.970
you know, things have
gone really well for me.

00:46:02.970 --> 00:46:03.810
You know what I saying?

00:46:03.810 --> 00:46:05.090
Things have gone really well for us.

00:46:05.090 --> 00:46:07.100
Things have also gone
well for me individually.

00:46:07.100 --> 00:46:09.990
Before we ever started doing what we did,

00:46:09.990 --> 00:46:12.180
it was just like I kind of prided myself

00:46:12.180 --> 00:46:13.013
on being good at stuff, right?

00:46:13.013 --> 00:46:15.130
It's like okay, I'm
gonna make good grades.

00:46:15.130 --> 00:46:16.709
I'm gonna be good at basketball.

00:46:16.709 --> 00:46:19.780
I mean, the things that I choose to do,

00:46:19.780 --> 00:46:21.960
I'm gonna do them well, right?

00:46:21.960 --> 00:46:23.360
And,

00:46:23.360 --> 00:46:27.090
so I kind of developed this sense of self

00:46:27.090 --> 00:46:29.883
that was based on being good at things.

00:46:30.810 --> 00:46:34.690
And then when it keeps
happening, when you keep trying

00:46:34.690 --> 00:46:36.730
and of course, there's lots of
failures, there's rejections

00:46:36.730 --> 00:46:40.300
like those relational, I
wouldn't say that dating

00:46:40.300 --> 00:46:41.690
was one of the things I was good at

00:46:41.690 --> 00:46:43.370
so I'll just put that out there.

00:46:43.370 --> 00:46:45.960
I did really well one time with my wife

00:46:45.960 --> 00:46:48.590
and things have worked out
really well since then.

00:46:50.290 --> 00:46:54.440
But this idea, I think the
thing that was so difficult

00:46:54.440 --> 00:46:59.440
about GMM 22, it was because
such a public failure

00:47:01.476 --> 00:47:04.927
so many people saw it as
you guys tried something

00:47:04.927 --> 00:47:09.180
and it did not work and it's
so obvious to everybody.

00:47:09.180 --> 00:47:11.090
Why don't you just admit now-

00:47:11.090 --> 00:47:12.940
- Isn't that embarrassing?
- Yeah.

00:47:12.940 --> 00:47:16.430
And again, there's this
you start, if you're me,

00:47:16.430 --> 00:47:21.430
you start, you've placed some
of your own personal value

00:47:21.620 --> 00:47:25.240
in the way that what
you've created is received.

00:47:25.240 --> 00:47:26.073
Right?

00:47:26.073 --> 00:47:27.620
And so I told you last week,

00:47:27.620 --> 00:47:30.870
my recommendation was Elizabeth
Gilbert's "Big Magic,"

00:47:30.870 --> 00:47:33.570
which I was talking to
my therapist about that

00:47:33.570 --> 00:47:34.490
and he was like, "Oh, you know,

00:47:34.490 --> 00:47:37.660
I used to recommend to a
lot of people her TED Talk."

00:47:37.660 --> 00:47:40.430
which if you don't want to read the book,

00:47:40.430 --> 00:47:42.187
which I do recommend the book,

00:47:42.187 --> 00:47:44.520
you can kind of get a
lot of the same ideas,

00:47:44.520 --> 00:47:46.060
and she's actually done a number

00:47:46.060 --> 00:47:47.380
of really popular TED Talks,

00:47:47.380 --> 00:47:49.093
where she talks about this.

00:47:50.010 --> 00:47:53.570
But she kind of emphasizes this idea

00:47:53.570 --> 00:47:58.270
of what you create matters a whole lot

00:47:58.270 --> 00:48:00.120
but also doesn't matter at all, right?

00:48:00.120 --> 00:48:02.220
There's this sort of built-in irony

00:48:02.220 --> 00:48:04.870
like it's sacred and it's awesome

00:48:04.870 --> 00:48:08.800
and creative creative expression
is an incredible thing,

00:48:08.800 --> 00:48:13.800
but also you kind of have to
live as if it doesn't matter,

00:48:15.240 --> 00:48:18.300
especially how it's received.

00:48:18.300 --> 00:48:19.283
The thing that you can't control.

00:48:19.283 --> 00:48:20.827
The thing you can control
is what you create

00:48:20.827 --> 00:48:21.708
and how you create it.

00:48:21.708 --> 00:48:24.695
The thing you cannot
control is this reception.

00:48:24.695 --> 00:48:27.940
And we operate in this world where

00:48:29.520 --> 00:48:34.410
reception is such a big piece of the pie

00:48:34.410 --> 00:48:36.940
because we throw things
out there constantly.

00:48:36.940 --> 00:48:38.880
We're throwing things out there to this,

00:48:38.880 --> 00:48:43.248
the most interactive media
genre that's ever existed.

00:48:43.248 --> 00:48:46.710
You know, what has happened
since YouTube, right?

00:48:46.710 --> 00:48:50.490
So immediate feedback, and
also this high quantity

00:48:50.490 --> 00:48:51.670
of content that's going out there.

00:48:51.670 --> 00:48:53.160
So you're just getting
this constant feedback

00:48:53.160 --> 00:48:54.540
that shaping everything.

00:48:54.540 --> 00:48:56.930
And so that's been very difficult for me,

00:48:56.930 --> 00:49:00.900
a performance-based person,
to create in a medium

00:49:00.900 --> 00:49:02.993
where approval,

00:49:04.160 --> 00:49:06.730
literally like how many people like this,

00:49:06.730 --> 00:49:10.730
how many people watch it,
the approval is quantifiable.

00:49:10.730 --> 00:49:13.460
- And it's a reflection in your mind

00:49:13.460 --> 00:49:15.410
of what people think of you,

00:49:15.410 --> 00:49:17.650
their assessment of you as a person.

00:49:17.650 --> 00:49:20.630
Oh, you must not be as
smart as I thought you were,

00:49:20.630 --> 00:49:22.870
Rhett and Link, because
you made a decision

00:49:22.870 --> 00:49:25.150
that led to something
that was embarrassing.

00:49:25.150 --> 00:49:26.390
- Right.
- That was a failure.

00:49:26.390 --> 00:49:30.120
You know, I think the
thing for us though is

00:49:30.120 --> 00:49:35.120
because we have this longstanding
and growing relationship

00:49:36.460 --> 00:49:38.220
with our audience,

00:49:38.220 --> 00:49:42.690
that it created a soft
place for us to fall.

00:49:42.690 --> 00:49:43.990
You know, where...

00:49:46.098 --> 00:49:51.098
I think any anybody who
listens to this podcast weekly

00:49:52.650 --> 00:49:57.130
has a sense of who we are and is on board.

00:49:57.130 --> 00:50:01.370
We have a, you know,
there's an understanding

00:50:01.370 --> 00:50:06.200
and there's a belief in, "Hey,
I might, I like these guys,

00:50:06.200 --> 00:50:08.525
you know, I'm loyal to these guys."

00:50:08.525 --> 00:50:09.374
- If you stick around.

00:50:09.374 --> 00:50:12.780
- Even if some things that they create

00:50:12.780 --> 00:50:15.950
aren't the best thing for me.

00:50:15.950 --> 00:50:18.459
Like, you may have a favorite artist

00:50:18.459 --> 00:50:21.360
who has a body of work
that from album to album,

00:50:21.360 --> 00:50:24.480
and you may not like every
single album the same,

00:50:24.480 --> 00:50:25.780
like Sturgill Simpson.

00:50:25.780 --> 00:50:29.390
Like I feel I have a
relationship with his work,

00:50:29.390 --> 00:50:32.490
not with him that is, you know,

00:50:32.490 --> 00:50:33.940
it's special to me

00:50:34.810 --> 00:50:36.610
but there are certain albums

00:50:36.610 --> 00:50:38.710
that like I really applaud the fact

00:50:38.710 --> 00:50:42.530
that he's doing his thing
and it wasn't for me.

00:50:42.530 --> 00:50:45.713
And there's been a few
of those albums. Right?

00:50:46.955 --> 00:50:48.963
But I still,

00:50:50.000 --> 00:50:52.197
I still believe in him and like give him,

00:50:52.197 --> 00:50:53.410
and those weren't failures.

00:50:53.410 --> 00:50:54.460
They just weren't for me.

00:50:54.460 --> 00:50:55.660
But even if they were failures,

00:50:55.660 --> 00:50:58.190
I'm like, "Hey, you know, I'm still a fan

00:50:58.190 --> 00:51:00.640
and I respect the fact that
just because I don't like it,

00:51:00.640 --> 00:51:02.920
or even if people didn't like it,

00:51:02.920 --> 00:51:06.049
that he was doing his thing
and I'm on board for that."

00:51:06.049 --> 00:51:09.340
And we have the benefit

00:51:09.340 --> 00:51:11.280
of having that soft place to fall

00:51:11.280 --> 00:51:15.300
because we have that loyalty
and that relationship built.

00:51:15.300 --> 00:51:16.500
There's, you know,

00:51:16.500 --> 00:51:19.070
I was reading Questlove's creativity book,

00:51:19.070 --> 00:51:21.460
which spoiler alert,
I'll recommend at the end

00:51:21.460 --> 00:51:22.910
and give you details about it.

00:51:22.910 --> 00:51:27.030
But he's talking about, he
talked about a creative failure

00:51:28.837 --> 00:51:30.127
and the type of failure

00:51:30.127 --> 00:51:32.240
that like if you put
something out into a world

00:51:32.240 --> 00:51:36.210
and nobody notices or cares,
and he was spinning that

00:51:36.210 --> 00:51:39.830
in a positive way as
there's freedom in that.

00:51:39.830 --> 00:51:41.690
There's freedom in that to do something,

00:51:41.690 --> 00:51:42.990
there's freedom that we don't have

00:51:42.990 --> 00:51:45.840
because everything we do
because of our success

00:51:45.840 --> 00:51:49.950
has a level of scrutiny that

00:51:49.950 --> 00:51:53.620
if you're a developing artist

00:51:53.620 --> 00:51:56.593
and you do something that doesn't work,

00:51:57.613 --> 00:52:01.350
you can focus on the freedom
to then try something new

00:52:01.350 --> 00:52:06.350
and not be under the thumb
of people's expectations.

00:52:07.090 --> 00:52:11.263
I think there's a way
to redeem every failure

00:52:11.263 --> 00:52:13.360
or every rejection.

00:52:13.360 --> 00:52:15.333
And I definitely think that's,

00:52:17.800 --> 00:52:19.890
finding, there's always,

00:52:19.890 --> 00:52:22.180
you can always see it as an
opportunity if you want to,

00:52:22.180 --> 00:52:26.550
you can always spin and
focus on the positive

00:52:26.550 --> 00:52:28.440
and you can learn things from it.

00:52:28.440 --> 00:52:32.140
You know, if it builds into who you are

00:52:32.140 --> 00:52:36.420
and changes who you are,
then it was worth it.

00:52:36.420 --> 00:52:37.253
Right?

00:52:38.418 --> 00:52:40.030
And it enables you to move forward

00:52:40.030 --> 00:52:44.020
as you don't allow it to
keep you from moving forward.

00:52:44.020 --> 00:52:48.373
And I definitely feel that if we,

00:52:49.730 --> 00:52:52.520
yeah, as we talk about GMM 22 now

00:52:52.520 --> 00:52:55.570
and if we continue to talk
about it and we're not,

00:52:55.570 --> 00:52:57.200
'cause I'm just not that interested in it.

00:52:57.200 --> 00:52:59.690
But I'm interested in talking about

00:52:59.690 --> 00:53:02.070
some other failures and rejections,

00:53:02.070 --> 00:53:04.460
our conversation would
continue to be different.

00:53:04.460 --> 00:53:06.580
Like so all the stuff that you said,

00:53:06.580 --> 00:53:10.220
and that I would say if we were
continuing to talk about it,

00:53:10.220 --> 00:53:11.560
will be different than what we were saying

00:53:11.560 --> 00:53:12.410
at that point in time

00:53:12.410 --> 00:53:15.420
and we were still really reeling from it.

00:53:15.420 --> 00:53:16.253
You know?

00:53:17.492 --> 00:53:19.582
And that may be the biggest takeaway,

00:53:19.582 --> 00:53:23.403
that the experience made us,

00:53:24.770 --> 00:53:27.208
maybe maturity's the right word or-

00:53:27.208 --> 00:53:28.833
- Well, we had the, you know,

00:53:30.350 --> 00:53:32.560
we have the benefit of

00:53:33.490 --> 00:53:36.030
being able to kind of
get back on the horse

00:53:36.030 --> 00:53:37.583
and throw something
else out into the world.

00:53:37.583 --> 00:53:39.760
It's kind of what you're
saying, a soft place to land.

00:53:39.760 --> 00:53:40.910
You've got an audience.

00:53:41.790 --> 00:53:44.120
It's not like when we
made a television show

00:53:44.120 --> 00:53:45.365
that got one season, which we can talk,

00:53:45.365 --> 00:53:49.615
our two TV things, both our
very first foray in 2007

00:53:49.615 --> 00:53:53.430
as hosts, that show was short-lived.

00:53:53.430 --> 00:53:56.340
And then the show that
we actually helped make

00:53:56.340 --> 00:53:58.200
and create that was much more about us,

00:53:58.200 --> 00:54:00.430
got one season.
- Commercial Kings.

00:54:00.430 --> 00:54:03.940
- And then it's like you
can't just turn around

00:54:03.940 --> 00:54:05.410
and churn out another television show,

00:54:05.410 --> 00:54:07.040
but you can turn to the internet

00:54:07.040 --> 00:54:08.490
and turn to a faithful audience

00:54:08.490 --> 00:54:11.820
so we do have this ability to kind of,

00:54:11.820 --> 00:54:16.580
we have this distinct advantage
of not having to sort of sit

00:54:16.580 --> 00:54:18.990
in our failure and not be able
to move forward creatively.

00:54:18.990 --> 00:54:22.450
And one of the things that
Elizabeth Gilbert talks about

00:54:22.450 --> 00:54:23.610
and this just may be in a book,

00:54:23.610 --> 00:54:25.996
but it's definitely one of the TED Talks,

00:54:25.996 --> 00:54:29.570
but she talks about how, you know,

00:54:29.570 --> 00:54:33.370
she had the incredible success
of writing "Eat, Pray, Love"

00:54:33.370 --> 00:54:34.620
which got turned into a movie.

00:54:34.620 --> 00:54:36.310
I haven't read the book or seen the movie.

00:54:36.310 --> 00:54:38.320
I don't necessarily plan
to, I'm not sure it's for me

00:54:38.320 --> 00:54:40.100
but it was a huge success.

00:54:40.100 --> 00:54:44.403
It was on a bestseller
list for like three years.

00:54:45.720 --> 00:54:48.610
And then she experienced
this incredible success

00:54:48.610 --> 00:54:49.720
and then there was all this pressure

00:54:49.720 --> 00:54:51.700
on writing her next book,

00:54:51.700 --> 00:54:53.810
and she wrote her next book and it bombed.

00:54:53.810 --> 00:54:55.110
And she was like "Thank goodness.

00:54:55.110 --> 00:54:56.497
I got that out of the way."

00:54:57.510 --> 00:55:01.750
Unlike Harper Lee who she
talks about in "Big Magic,"

00:55:01.750 --> 00:55:02.970
who wrote "To Kill a Mockingbird"

00:55:02.970 --> 00:55:04.760
and then didn't write anything else.

00:55:04.760 --> 00:55:07.040
And she didn't write anything
else, because she was scared

00:55:07.040 --> 00:55:10.100
of not being able to repeat the success

00:55:10.100 --> 00:55:11.439
of "To Kill a Mockingbird".

00:55:11.439 --> 00:55:13.687
- I thought there was
a hidden novel though.

00:55:13.687 --> 00:55:16.180
- No, she did, something
else was released later

00:55:16.180 --> 00:55:17.680
but I'm saying for a long
time, she didn't write.

00:55:17.680 --> 00:55:21.721
- Yeah, but it wasn't,
Harper Lee did not publish it

00:55:21.721 --> 00:55:23.370
I don't think.
- Right. She was so scared.

00:55:23.370 --> 00:55:26.480
So one of the things that she talks about

00:55:26.480 --> 00:55:30.460
is how you've got this
home position, right?

00:55:30.460 --> 00:55:35.330
And then success throws you away from home

00:55:35.330 --> 00:55:37.640
and failure throws you away from home,

00:55:37.640 --> 00:55:42.640
but emotionally you don't really
have the cognitive ability

00:55:42.650 --> 00:55:45.300
to feel the difference.

00:55:45.300 --> 00:55:48.390
In other words, success
can be just as disorienting

00:55:48.390 --> 00:55:50.119
as failure can, right?

00:55:50.119 --> 00:55:55.119
And it also, it goes to your
head in a similar way, right?

00:55:55.650 --> 00:56:00.650
So success can breed fear,
failure can breed fear.

00:56:00.756 --> 00:56:03.500
And she's like whether
you are super successful

00:56:03.500 --> 00:56:06.600
with something or it's
something is a huge flop,

00:56:06.600 --> 00:56:09.010
the goal for her is to return home,

00:56:09.010 --> 00:56:10.530
is to return to home base.

00:56:10.530 --> 00:56:13.980
And for her home is doing the thing

00:56:13.980 --> 00:56:18.570
that she would do anyway,
if success or failure,

00:56:18.570 --> 00:56:20.520
you know, whether people
are watching or not

00:56:20.520 --> 00:56:22.220
and that's writing for her, right.

00:56:23.130 --> 00:56:24.210
It's simple for her.

00:56:24.210 --> 00:56:25.410
She's gonna go back to writing

00:56:25.410 --> 00:56:26.380
and that's what she's gonna do

00:56:26.380 --> 00:56:28.050
whether or not you're buying it or not.

00:56:28.050 --> 00:56:33.050
And I think that now, our
creative process is complicated.

00:56:33.420 --> 00:56:34.960
It's not, we're not just writing

00:56:34.960 --> 00:56:36.750
we're doing a bunch of different things.

00:56:36.750 --> 00:56:40.470
And also the larger, the more
success that we've gotten

00:56:40.470 --> 00:56:42.370
and the bigger our company has become,

00:56:43.259 --> 00:56:45.953
there's more people depending on the stuff

00:56:45.953 --> 00:56:48.630
that we come up with working

00:56:48.630 --> 00:56:50.700
than there were 10 years ago.

00:56:50.700 --> 00:56:51.533
Right?

00:56:51.533 --> 00:56:52.540
So now it's like, okay, now we've got,

00:56:52.540 --> 00:56:55.110
there's people's livelihoods
are on the line, right?

00:56:55.110 --> 00:56:57.370
There's a company, there's employees.

00:56:57.370 --> 00:57:00.357
And so something working
isn't just about us

00:57:00.357 --> 00:57:03.350
and our like personal
creative fulfillment.

00:57:03.350 --> 00:57:05.629
So it's a little bit complicated,

00:57:05.629 --> 00:57:08.290
but the advantage we have
with being able to turn around

00:57:08.290 --> 00:57:09.123
and just be like, well,

00:57:09.123 --> 00:57:11.270
there's gonna be another video on Monday.

00:57:11.270 --> 00:57:13.380
There's gonna be another
podcast next week.

00:57:13.380 --> 00:57:15.060
There's gonna be another,

00:57:15.060 --> 00:57:16.970
we're gonna right back
to the drawing board

00:57:16.970 --> 00:57:18.329
and start writing another
pilot for something

00:57:18.329 --> 00:57:22.400
or writing a movie, is that
our home is pretty clear.

00:57:22.400 --> 00:57:24.660
We always know where to come back to

00:57:24.660 --> 00:57:27.500
and that's just coming back
to the creative process

00:57:27.500 --> 00:57:29.350
and just churn something else out.

00:57:29.350 --> 00:57:31.900
- But specifically, Good Mythical Morning

00:57:31.900 --> 00:57:35.360
and Ear Biscuits being kind
of like a creative home base

00:57:35.360 --> 00:57:38.840
where it's properties that are working,

00:57:38.840 --> 00:57:43.840
where we're reaching you
listening or watching.

00:57:43.890 --> 00:57:44.723
You know?

00:57:45.914 --> 00:57:48.970
It's quite a luxury, especially because

00:57:48.970 --> 00:57:51.410
we've been able to structure
our professional lives

00:57:51.410 --> 00:57:54.887
to then spend a good amount
of time on speculative stuff.

00:57:54.887 --> 00:57:57.140
And I do want to get back to
that and reference the stuff

00:57:57.140 --> 00:57:58.390
we talked about last week,

00:57:58.390 --> 00:58:02.307
everything that fizzled
out was rejected or failed.

00:58:03.830 --> 00:58:08.830
You know, it's difficult for
me because I'm more of an,

00:58:10.790 --> 00:58:13.530
you know, I thrive more
in the execution phase

00:58:13.530 --> 00:58:14.370
and I don't mean the killing,

00:58:14.370 --> 00:58:17.500
but I'm meaning carrying
out of the creative process

00:58:17.500 --> 00:58:20.673
then in the ideation
and the writing of it,

00:58:21.860 --> 00:58:25.300
which is a point that you thrive in.

00:58:25.300 --> 00:58:29.063
So I'm actually curious
how you interact with,

00:58:29.930 --> 00:58:33.510
you know, a premature rejection.

00:58:33.510 --> 00:58:36.350
Like we write a pitch,

00:58:36.350 --> 00:58:39.100
we write five pitches on a
page like we read last week,

00:58:39.100 --> 00:58:41.710
and then, you know,

00:58:41.710 --> 00:58:44.450
maybe none of them,
nobody's excited about it.

00:58:44.450 --> 00:58:45.710
So then we all say, "Oh crap.

00:58:45.710 --> 00:58:47.300
We'll just put that in a Google Drive

00:58:47.300 --> 00:58:51.010
and read it on Ear Biscuits
years later and laugh about it."

00:58:51.010 --> 00:58:52.220
But in the moment it's like, ah,

00:58:52.220 --> 00:58:53.450
gotta start over.

00:58:53.450 --> 00:58:55.400
Already starting over.

00:58:55.400 --> 00:58:59.270
And I'm sitting on my
hands like, oh, okay.

00:58:59.270 --> 00:59:00.300
Gotta start over.

00:59:00.300 --> 00:59:03.830
You know, or we are writing,

00:59:03.830 --> 00:59:07.600
we decide to go write a pilot
and spend that amount of time

00:59:07.600 --> 00:59:11.510
to invest in that, knowing that

00:59:11.510 --> 00:59:15.730
because it's a foray into
traditional entertainment,

00:59:15.730 --> 00:59:17.930
it's skipping tracks for us.

00:59:17.930 --> 00:59:20.230
And we have this, you know,

00:59:20.230 --> 00:59:21.720
it's something that we really want.

00:59:21.720 --> 00:59:23.233
We want to,

00:59:24.773 --> 00:59:27.180
we want to stretch our arms

00:59:27.180 --> 00:59:29.800
and to build our muscles creatively

00:59:29.800 --> 00:59:34.260
and take some swings in other places

00:59:34.260 --> 00:59:38.470
just to to get those type of
things out into the world,

00:59:38.470 --> 00:59:40.440
just to make that type of stuff,

00:59:40.440 --> 00:59:44.253
to have that challenge and that
experience and that reward.

00:59:45.400 --> 00:59:47.550
But we know that

00:59:49.120 --> 00:59:50.920
we're not as special.

00:59:50.920 --> 00:59:53.673
In fact, what we discover is that,

00:59:54.530 --> 00:59:57.740
I guess in most people's
ideas and these gatekeepers,

00:59:57.740 --> 01:00:01.990
like the things that make us special

01:00:01.990 --> 01:00:03.973
or at least the popularity,

01:00:05.558 --> 01:00:06.897
and the momentum that we have.

01:00:06.897 --> 01:00:07.730
(clears throat)
Excuse me.

01:00:07.730 --> 01:00:09.190
And the success that we have.

01:00:09.190 --> 01:00:11.393
- Not an asset.
- It just doesn't seem,

01:00:12.500 --> 01:00:13.800
it gets us in the room

01:00:13.800 --> 01:00:16.450
but it doesn't necessarily
get things made.

01:00:16.450 --> 01:00:18.650
- And I would go as far as to say

01:00:18.650 --> 01:00:20.800
it's not an asset, it's a liability.

01:00:20.800 --> 01:00:21.633
- Hmm. Yeah.

01:00:21.633 --> 01:00:23.930
I mean, we at least start to question that

01:00:23.930 --> 01:00:25.500
because it's like, okay,

01:00:25.500 --> 01:00:27.158
we can get in the room, but is it, okay,

01:00:27.158 --> 01:00:31.100
are our ideas and what we've written

01:00:31.100 --> 01:00:35.200
and what our pitch is,
are they not good enough?

01:00:35.200 --> 01:00:37.649
Well, there's other reasons

01:00:37.649 --> 01:00:39.960
that don't have anything
to do with who we are

01:00:39.960 --> 01:00:43.470
and like our ability
and talent as artists,

01:00:43.470 --> 01:00:44.560
but it is a part of it.

01:00:44.560 --> 01:00:46.530
So it's like, you start,
you can get in your own head

01:00:46.530 --> 01:00:47.893
about that type of stuff.

01:00:50.750 --> 01:00:53.836
Because you pitch in the
room and they're like,

01:00:53.836 --> 01:00:56.630
there's a million reasons to say no

01:00:56.630 --> 01:00:59.440
and there seems to only be
a few reasons to say yes.

01:00:59.440 --> 01:01:02.890
And then even a yes to a pilot is some,

01:01:02.890 --> 01:01:06.713
it's an unknown length process to then,

01:01:07.846 --> 01:01:11.140
and it's a crap shoot that
something ever gets made.

01:01:11.140 --> 01:01:16.010
So it's like, it's setting
us up for a beating

01:01:16.010 --> 01:01:18.960
and so even though we're so successful,

01:01:18.960 --> 01:01:21.840
we focus on what we want to go after.

01:01:21.840 --> 01:01:25.250
And we do have this experience
of taking a beating,

01:01:25.250 --> 01:01:26.248
you know?

01:01:26.248 --> 01:01:30.440
So how do we deal with that?

01:01:30.440 --> 01:01:33.590
And 'cause we keep signing up for it

01:01:33.590 --> 01:01:35.880
and it's more frustrating for us

01:01:35.880 --> 01:01:38.950
because we know the
experience of making something

01:01:38.950 --> 01:01:41.210
and have it and people seeing it

01:01:41.210 --> 01:01:43.476
and people liking it, you know?

01:01:43.476 --> 01:01:47.280
Or if they don't, it's
a soft place to fall.

01:01:47.280 --> 01:01:49.210
We're spoiled. Right?

01:01:49.210 --> 01:01:51.200
But we're trying to switch tracks

01:01:51.200 --> 01:01:52.910
and do something different.

01:01:52.910 --> 01:01:53.743
I mean,

01:01:56.410 --> 01:01:58.260
so I'll let you respond to that

01:01:58.260 --> 01:02:00.110
and then I'll talk about how

01:02:02.500 --> 01:02:06.600
like I've tried to adjust
my approach mentally

01:02:06.600 --> 01:02:08.340
to this entire process.

01:02:08.340 --> 01:02:10.913
- I mean, I'm thinking a lot of things.

01:02:13.750 --> 01:02:17.023
You know, I definitely,

01:02:20.910 --> 01:02:22.480
there is a part of me that

01:02:23.530 --> 01:02:25.126
I've always kind of had this belief

01:02:25.126 --> 01:02:29.149
that's not a realistic belief
that we can do anything.

01:02:29.149 --> 01:02:30.440
Right? (laughing)

01:02:30.440 --> 01:02:31.273
That if we-

01:02:31.273 --> 01:02:32.690
- It's kind of like you can date anybody.

01:02:32.690 --> 01:02:35.830
- Yeah, if you set your mind to something,

01:02:35.830 --> 01:02:36.900
we can figure this out.

01:02:36.900 --> 01:02:38.031
We can write a book.

01:02:38.031 --> 01:02:39.290
We can write a movie.

01:02:39.290 --> 01:02:40.786
We can make music.

01:02:40.786 --> 01:02:44.930
We can, you know, and so we
have done a lot of those things

01:02:44.930 --> 01:02:49.180
because there's just
this persistent belief

01:02:49.180 --> 01:02:51.060
that we can do it.

01:02:51.060 --> 01:02:51.893
'Cause I'm always like, well,

01:02:51.893 --> 01:02:54.470
somebody figured it out, you know?

01:02:54.470 --> 01:02:57.370
And the funny thing is
that in "Big Magic,"

01:02:57.370 --> 01:02:58.500
she kind of talks...

01:02:58.500 --> 01:03:00.227
One of the things I always think that,

01:03:00.227 --> 01:03:02.750
when I'm writing for instance

01:03:03.630 --> 01:03:05.460
and I'm learning a lot while I'm writing

01:03:05.460 --> 01:03:07.160
and especially if we have the privilege

01:03:07.160 --> 01:03:09.190
of working with someone
who's more experienced

01:03:09.190 --> 01:03:11.920
like we did on a recent
thing that we developed

01:03:11.920 --> 01:03:14.370
that's still kind of
maybe gonna be something

01:03:14.370 --> 01:03:17.683
but probably not. (laughing)

01:03:19.050 --> 01:03:24.050
Is my lack of experience and
education in the creative arts

01:03:25.170 --> 01:03:27.930
becomes frustrating to me, right?

01:03:27.930 --> 01:03:31.635
Now, meaning that I went to
engineering school, right?

01:03:31.635 --> 01:03:36.630
I didn't even pay attention
in any of my creative classes.

01:03:36.630 --> 01:03:38.660
I didn't make the connection

01:03:38.660 --> 01:03:40.880
between how there was an opportunity

01:03:40.880 --> 01:03:45.030
to pursue vocationally entertainment

01:03:45.030 --> 01:03:48.193
until YouTube came around.

01:03:49.499 --> 01:03:53.600
And so you start seeing
that like, okay, well,

01:03:53.600 --> 01:03:55.627
it isn't easy to just write something.

01:03:55.627 --> 01:03:58.980
It doesn't just come naturally.

01:03:58.980 --> 01:04:01.980
Like there are sort of,
there are some unspoken rules

01:04:01.980 --> 01:04:04.480
and there are rules that the people

01:04:04.480 --> 01:04:05.840
who are gonna be reading your scripts,

01:04:05.840 --> 01:04:07.340
no, they've been educated.

01:04:07.340 --> 01:04:08.480
They've read a lot of scripts.

01:04:08.480 --> 01:04:10.700
There's certain things
that they're looking for.

01:04:10.700 --> 01:04:14.920
Now, I'm not, you can get way
too formulaic in the process.

01:04:14.920 --> 01:04:17.580
But one of the things that
she talks about in "Big Magic"

01:04:17.580 --> 01:04:21.670
is how that creative
education and academia

01:04:21.670 --> 01:04:24.210
is kind of overrated, right?

01:04:24.210 --> 01:04:27.310
Like the idea of storytelling

01:04:27.310 --> 01:04:31.070
in general is something
that is exists in our DNA

01:04:31.070 --> 01:04:34.980
as a species, and that this is who we are.

01:04:34.980 --> 01:04:36.770
And then I start questioning, well,

01:04:36.770 --> 01:04:39.270
do I have anything important to say?

01:04:39.270 --> 01:04:43.360
Do we have something that
people will care about?

01:04:43.360 --> 01:04:46.750
Is this original enough?

01:04:46.750 --> 01:04:49.408
You know, is this an original idea?

01:04:49.408 --> 01:04:51.947
Elizabeth says, you know,

01:04:51.947 --> 01:04:55.887
"Originality is secondary
to authenticity," right?

01:04:55.887 --> 01:04:58.740
And there's nothing, ultimately
there's nothing original.

01:04:58.740 --> 01:05:01.130
Like there's nothing new
under the sun, you know?

01:05:01.130 --> 01:05:02.750
Solomon had that figured out.

01:05:02.750 --> 01:05:07.070
And so you're going to do
something that has been done.

01:05:07.070 --> 01:05:08.370
You're gonna come up with a melody

01:05:08.370 --> 01:05:10.350
that kind of already exists.

01:05:10.350 --> 01:05:12.270
It's like there's only so
many combinations of notes.

01:05:12.270 --> 01:05:14.170
There's only so many expressions of ideas.

01:05:14.170 --> 01:05:15.930
There's only so many stories you can tell,

01:05:15.930 --> 01:05:20.930
but only you as an individual
can tell your specific story

01:05:21.640 --> 01:05:23.530
in your specific way.

01:05:23.530 --> 01:05:24.363
And so I, so that-

01:05:24.363 --> 01:05:28.960
- So this whole counteract
an inferiority complex

01:05:28.960 --> 01:05:33.370
or a fear of being exposed as

01:05:34.460 --> 01:05:38.200
like uneducated or untalented.

01:05:38.200 --> 01:05:39.784
- 'Cause everyone is faking it.

01:05:39.784 --> 01:05:41.250
When you think about it,

01:05:41.250 --> 01:05:45.260
the person that you're
pitching to is also faking it.

01:05:45.260 --> 01:05:47.470
They're trying to maintain their job.

01:05:47.470 --> 01:05:50.420
They don't want to get fired

01:05:50.420 --> 01:05:52.670
and they want to please the
person that they report to

01:05:52.670 --> 01:05:54.800
who wants to please the
person that they report to

01:05:54.800 --> 01:05:57.040
who wants to be the man
or whatever, you know?

01:05:57.040 --> 01:05:59.029
So it's like, everyone is kind,

01:05:59.029 --> 01:06:02.598
I'm not saying that technical
creative writing education

01:06:02.598 --> 01:06:03.487
is a bad thing.

01:06:03.487 --> 01:06:04.860
I think that would be great.

01:06:04.860 --> 01:06:07.210
I wish I had had had it honestly, right?

01:06:07.210 --> 01:06:08.740
Especially given what I'm doing now

01:06:08.740 --> 01:06:12.920
but when you kind of just are like listen,

01:06:12.920 --> 01:06:16.990
this doesn't really matter.

01:06:16.990 --> 01:06:20.900
And also if I'm not enjoying
every step of the process

01:06:20.900 --> 01:06:22.060
up to the point of rejection,

01:06:22.060 --> 01:06:24.145
which the good news is
I am, I enjoy writing.

01:06:24.145 --> 01:06:27.610
I enjoy the process of it creating a role

01:06:27.610 --> 01:06:28.770
or exploring things

01:06:28.770 --> 01:06:30.530
and putting words into characters mouths

01:06:30.530 --> 01:06:31.650
and that kind of thing.

01:06:31.650 --> 01:06:35.100
And every single time I do
it, I learn a whole lot.

01:06:35.100 --> 01:06:37.600
So I know the next time I
do it, it's gonna be better.

01:06:37.600 --> 01:06:39.500
And I'm probably gonna increase,

01:06:39.500 --> 01:06:41.140
the chances will be
increased the next time

01:06:41.140 --> 01:06:42.100
that this will come to light.

01:06:42.100 --> 01:06:45.790
But you know, what? If it
doesn't, it's just a lesson.

01:06:45.790 --> 01:06:47.442
It's just an experience.

01:06:47.442 --> 01:06:51.610
And it's what was supposed to happen.

01:06:51.610 --> 01:06:53.550
You know, even if that's not true,

01:06:53.550 --> 01:06:55.630
even if everything doesn't
happen for a reason,

01:06:55.630 --> 01:06:57.940
living like everything
happens for a reason

01:06:57.940 --> 01:06:59.650
is a healthy perspective.

01:06:59.650 --> 01:07:01.483
- I think for me,

01:07:02.630 --> 01:07:04.890
and this is something that I actually

01:07:04.890 --> 01:07:08.070
started saying out loud
as we were entering

01:07:08.070 --> 01:07:10.853
into certain processes.

01:07:10.853 --> 01:07:13.320
I'm thinking of two examples.

01:07:13.320 --> 01:07:14.650
One is

01:07:16.184 --> 01:07:20.550
we were doing a pitch and we
had teamed up with somebody

01:07:20.550 --> 01:07:24.253
who if the pitch went
as a television show,

01:07:24.253 --> 01:07:28.250
they will be running the,
they'd be the head writer

01:07:28.250 --> 01:07:30.080
and like show runner.

01:07:30.080 --> 01:07:33.680
And we were pitching that.

01:07:33.680 --> 01:07:36.320
And then the other one
is writing the pilot

01:07:36.320 --> 01:07:41.320
that we collaborated
with a friend and mentor.

01:07:41.530 --> 01:07:42.363
- Yeah.

01:07:42.363 --> 01:07:46.030
- So with the first one, I
remember we were going into a,

01:07:46.030 --> 01:07:48.760
well, it doesn't matter
which network, but you know,

01:07:48.760 --> 01:07:52.618
a bonafide meeting, big deal.

01:07:52.618 --> 01:07:54.063
By the way,

01:07:56.410 --> 01:08:00.430
our livelihood is not
connected to our pitches

01:08:00.430 --> 01:08:03.220
working for things being bought from us.

01:08:03.220 --> 01:08:08.070
Like we have this luxury
of not having this,

01:08:09.660 --> 01:08:11.580
oh gosh, I gotta, you know, this

01:08:12.780 --> 01:08:14.690
I gotta put food on the table.

01:08:14.690 --> 01:08:16.103
You know, we gotta make this work.

01:08:16.103 --> 01:08:17.390
- There's not a desperation.

01:08:17.390 --> 01:08:19.833
- There's not desperation,

01:08:21.560 --> 01:08:22.881
but we want it so bad that it feels

01:08:22.881 --> 01:08:24.487
the same at times.
- Yeah.

01:08:24.487 --> 01:08:26.805
- And I do think that
as you were getting at,

01:08:26.805 --> 01:08:29.790
that can short circuit

01:08:30.960 --> 01:08:34.200
your ability to pitch and
sell something I think

01:08:34.200 --> 01:08:36.870
when it's like you're gripping so tightly

01:08:36.870 --> 01:08:39.486
that you're like shaking or, you know?

01:08:39.486 --> 01:08:41.770
Sometimes literally.

01:08:41.770 --> 01:08:43.890
I remember we had done it,
we had gone around town

01:08:43.890 --> 01:08:46.950
pitching this thing and
this the last person

01:08:46.950 --> 01:08:48.790
we were pitching to.

01:08:48.790 --> 01:08:51.400
And remember, we were
sitting in the waiting room

01:08:51.400 --> 01:08:56.400
with the writer who we
paired ourselves with.

01:08:56.640 --> 01:08:59.710
And before we went back there,
you remember I said to him,

01:08:59.710 --> 01:09:01.793
I was like, "Well,

01:09:03.996 --> 01:09:06.682
you know what, this is our last meeting.

01:09:06.682 --> 01:09:09.055
If this doesn't go, we
may never see you again."

01:09:09.055 --> 01:09:10.180
(Rhett laughing)

01:09:10.180 --> 01:09:13.040
You know, we were introduced
to him for this project.

01:09:13.040 --> 01:09:14.210
We really hit it off.

01:09:14.210 --> 01:09:18.290
I think I could see us
being really good friends

01:09:18.290 --> 01:09:20.140
and collaborators

01:09:20.140 --> 01:09:23.433
and having an entire
creative future together,

01:09:24.990 --> 01:09:27.190
and that's one of the
reasons we picked the person.

01:09:27.190 --> 01:09:28.023
- Mm-hmm.

01:09:28.023 --> 01:09:29.810
- Or this could be the last time

01:09:29.810 --> 01:09:31.160
that we ever see each other.

01:09:31.160 --> 01:09:35.250
And there was like this kind
of uncomfortable laughter

01:09:35.250 --> 01:09:36.990
because we all knew it was true.

01:09:36.990 --> 01:09:37.823
Right?

01:09:37.823 --> 01:09:39.610
It's just not the type of
thing that you typically say

01:09:39.610 --> 01:09:41.820
unless your Link and
you're a little nervous

01:09:41.820 --> 01:09:44.171
or you're trying to figure
out something to talk about.

01:09:44.171 --> 01:09:46.610
And there's nothing like
a good dose of the truth

01:09:46.610 --> 01:09:49.850
to really shake you out of being nervous.

01:09:49.850 --> 01:09:53.030
And that's exactly what happened.

01:09:53.030 --> 01:09:56.420
That was over a year ago.

01:09:56.420 --> 01:09:58.820
And even without the COVID of it all,

01:09:58.820 --> 01:10:00.990
like we still would have
never seen him again.

01:10:00.990 --> 01:10:03.670
I mean, we might, who knows
if we'll ever cross paths

01:10:03.670 --> 01:10:05.340
but there's just like, you know,

01:10:05.340 --> 01:10:08.040
we could be working together for years

01:10:08.040 --> 01:10:09.590
or we could never see each other.

01:10:09.590 --> 01:10:10.423
Isn't that funny?

01:10:10.423 --> 01:10:12.067
- Yeah. And it's true.

01:10:12.067 --> 01:10:13.880
- And the reason why I said it is

01:10:13.880 --> 01:10:15.920
because I was trying to
adopt this philosophy

01:10:15.920 --> 01:10:19.430
that like all of this stuff is so tenuous.

01:10:19.430 --> 01:10:20.263
Like

01:10:22.250 --> 01:10:25.370
the least we can do is be
entertained by the process

01:10:25.370 --> 01:10:29.280
because it may not be ever
anything more than this.

01:10:29.280 --> 01:10:32.410
Like we go into this
pitch, we practiced it.

01:10:32.410 --> 01:10:34.663
We kind of know it backwards and forwards.

01:10:36.760 --> 01:10:37.780
Let's have fun with it.

01:10:37.780 --> 01:10:39.720
If all that happens with this project

01:10:39.720 --> 01:10:41.513
and it never goes past a pitch,

01:10:41.513 --> 01:10:44.400
I want to have fond
memories of this pitch,

01:10:44.400 --> 01:10:46.090
like I want to take the pressure off

01:10:46.090 --> 01:10:48.760
and just convey that we believe in it

01:10:48.760 --> 01:10:53.450
and leave the results to whatever power

01:10:53.450 --> 01:10:55.940
you want to leave the results to.

01:10:55.940 --> 01:10:59.257
So that's the thing
that I was trying to do

01:10:59.257 --> 01:11:01.300
was just embrace the process

01:11:01.300 --> 01:11:05.067
because the results
are out of our control.

01:11:05.067 --> 01:11:09.940
- Yeah, all we can do is we
can come up with an idea,

01:11:09.940 --> 01:11:14.041
we can write a story and we
can have a compelling pitch

01:11:14.041 --> 01:11:17.876
but it is so tenuous because the reason

01:11:17.876 --> 01:11:20.950
that someone might say no

01:11:20.950 --> 01:11:23.690
could very easily be
what they had for lunch.

01:11:23.690 --> 01:11:26.070
It could be a conversation that they had

01:11:26.070 --> 01:11:28.730
with their spouse right
before the meeting.

01:11:28.730 --> 01:11:32.550
- And that can be
frustrating or it can be fuel

01:11:32.550 --> 01:11:34.460
to let go of the things

01:11:34.460 --> 01:11:35.497
that you just have an illusion of control.

01:11:35.497 --> 01:11:38.450
- I think when you actually think about it

01:11:38.450 --> 01:11:41.820
and when you understand how tenuous it is,

01:11:41.820 --> 01:11:45.120
for me, it is helpful to
be like you know what?

01:11:45.120 --> 01:11:49.030
Why would I place my value,

01:11:49.030 --> 01:11:52.000
my happiness in the hands of someone

01:11:52.000 --> 01:11:53.526
who just had a bad lunch?

01:11:53.526 --> 01:11:54.359
(laughing)

01:11:54.359 --> 01:11:55.810
You know what I'm saying? Like what?

01:11:55.810 --> 01:11:57.520
And so again, it is frustrating

01:11:57.520 --> 01:12:00.220
when you take the, but I feel like for me

01:12:01.430 --> 01:12:04.640
step one is finding joy in the process,

01:12:04.640 --> 01:12:06.510
what you just said and that is everything

01:12:06.510 --> 01:12:09.660
from the conceptualization and the writing

01:12:09.660 --> 01:12:11.900
to going out and talking about it,

01:12:11.900 --> 01:12:14.110
meeting people, doing the pitches,

01:12:14.110 --> 01:12:16.090
having fun while we're doing it.

01:12:16.090 --> 01:12:19.310
And then the second thing
is, and this is not easy,

01:12:19.310 --> 01:12:20.360
I want to be very clear.

01:12:20.360 --> 01:12:21.560
This isn't easy for anybody.

01:12:21.560 --> 01:12:22.930
It's definitely not easy for me,

01:12:22.930 --> 01:12:25.820
somebody who built an identity on

01:12:26.856 --> 01:12:28.457
impressing people .

01:12:30.140 --> 01:12:33.920
To take all the power,

01:12:33.920 --> 01:12:37.957
to give the results,

01:12:37.957 --> 01:12:40.640
my expectations, there's no power in that.

01:12:40.640 --> 01:12:44.407
I'm just removing all my
expectations altogether

01:12:46.130 --> 01:12:48.748
and trying to detach
myself from the outcome.

01:12:48.748 --> 01:12:51.030
If I can enjoy the process

01:12:51.030 --> 01:12:53.250
and detach myself from the outcome,

01:12:53.250 --> 01:12:55.760
and again, there's things
that make that easier for us

01:12:55.760 --> 01:12:58.170
given the fact that it
doesn't have to work.

01:12:58.170 --> 01:13:02.010
Like we can go back, we can
keep doing what we're doing.

01:13:02.010 --> 01:13:03.670
It's we're gonna be okay

01:13:03.670 --> 01:13:06.080
in the short term financially, you kno?w

01:13:07.210 --> 01:13:10.820
We're not depending on this
working in order to eat.

01:13:10.820 --> 01:13:12.350
- Yeah.

01:13:12.350 --> 01:13:14.050
- So those are the, and again,

01:13:14.050 --> 01:13:16.640
and if that's your situation, again,

01:13:16.640 --> 01:13:20.420
I know I'm biting a lot of
Elizabeth Gilbert's thoughts here

01:13:20.420 --> 01:13:22.070
but I mean, she kind of makes the point

01:13:22.070 --> 01:13:26.390
that because the process,

01:13:26.390 --> 01:13:29.743
the arts are so tenuous
and because people,

01:13:30.590 --> 01:13:34.040
you're putting your
livelihood into the whims

01:13:34.040 --> 01:13:36.473
of another person who
holds the purse strings,

01:13:37.690 --> 01:13:40.110
she recommends don't quit your day job.

01:13:40.110 --> 01:13:42.550
I know that sounds harsh,

01:13:42.550 --> 01:13:47.550
but she's like she didn't
stop working her day job

01:13:47.660 --> 01:13:50.810
until after she published,
"Eat, Pray, Love"

01:13:50.810 --> 01:13:53.200
and it started seeming
like it was working.

01:13:53.200 --> 01:13:56.460
Like she had all these different jobs

01:13:56.460 --> 01:14:00.870
and she was like make yourself,

01:14:00.870 --> 01:14:02.970
give yourself that creative time,

01:14:02.970 --> 01:14:06.050
but the moment that you
begin to attach yourself

01:14:06.050 --> 01:14:07.370
and attach your self worth

01:14:07.370 --> 01:14:11.053
and attach your own livelihood to it,

01:14:12.240 --> 01:14:13.760
it becomes a different thing.

01:14:13.760 --> 01:14:15.670
Now we had the privilege of, okay, well,

01:14:15.670 --> 01:14:18.190
the baseline creativity stuff is working.

01:14:18.190 --> 01:14:21.440
So we have this privilege of
being able to kind of do that,

01:14:21.440 --> 01:14:22.860
to put these things out into the world

01:14:22.860 --> 01:14:23.988
and not have them have to work.

01:14:23.988 --> 01:14:28.020
- Yeah and with that second example of

01:14:30.460 --> 01:14:33.010
co-writing a pilot was with somebody

01:14:33.010 --> 01:14:35.860
with a lot more experience
that can give, you know,

01:14:35.860 --> 01:14:40.770
just being on Zoom calls
and having that time

01:14:40.770 --> 01:14:42.770
it was just like, you know, this is just,

01:14:42.770 --> 01:14:44.900
the thing that I would remind myself

01:14:44.900 --> 01:14:47.580
and just I would say out loud at times

01:14:47.580 --> 01:14:50.360
to all of us on the
Zoom call is just like,

01:14:50.360 --> 01:14:53.870
you know, like you said,
this may never be anything,

01:14:53.870 --> 01:14:58.870
but it's fun to think
about what it could be.

01:14:59.270 --> 01:15:01.880
And this is fun right now.

01:15:01.880 --> 01:15:03.630
Like this experience is fun.

01:15:03.630 --> 01:15:07.810
Like taking that moment
to pause and recognize,

01:15:07.810 --> 01:15:09.560
oh, we just got notes,

01:15:09.560 --> 01:15:13.720
but the way he gives notes
is he records it all,

01:15:13.720 --> 01:15:15.470
audio stream of consciousness

01:15:15.470 --> 01:15:17.760
and then he just emails it to you

01:15:17.760 --> 01:15:22.410
so like you've got this
like verbal processing

01:15:22.410 --> 01:15:27.220
of what we've conceptualized
and what you've written.

01:15:27.220 --> 01:15:30.370
And there's like this
detailed analysis of it

01:15:30.370 --> 01:15:32.793
that like people would pay,

01:15:33.770 --> 01:15:37.775
people would save up for years
to get that type of feedback

01:15:37.775 --> 01:15:41.630
on something they've
written from someone of

01:15:41.630 --> 01:15:43.250
this level of experience.

01:15:43.250 --> 01:15:45.333
And just saying, okay,

01:15:48.060 --> 01:15:49.805
the notes might eviscerate.

01:15:49.805 --> 01:15:51.640
And they didn't,

01:15:51.640 --> 01:15:54.040
but even if they did, it's like, wow,

01:15:54.040 --> 01:15:55.650
what a cool process.

01:15:55.650 --> 01:15:58.910
It's like signing up
for your driving course

01:15:58.910 --> 01:16:00.560
and then you get to the parking lot

01:16:00.560 --> 01:16:02.470
and Mr. Miyagi gets in the car with you

01:16:02.470 --> 01:16:03.570
with a glass of wine.

01:16:03.570 --> 01:16:07.250
It's like, oh God, this is freaking cool.

01:16:07.250 --> 01:16:09.420
I need to go home and
watch "Karate Kid" now

01:16:09.420 --> 01:16:12.230
and I think I would really,
really appreciate this.

01:16:12.230 --> 01:16:13.307
- Yeah.
- You know what I'm saying?

01:16:13.307 --> 01:16:14.560
- And it's tough do that.

01:16:14.560 --> 01:16:16.630
It's tough to enjoy the process

01:16:16.630 --> 01:16:19.720
because I think there is a,

01:16:19.720 --> 01:16:23.170
there's this romantic notion
of the starving artist

01:16:23.170 --> 01:16:27.850
and the desperate artist
and the tortured artist,

01:16:27.850 --> 01:16:30.910
you know, literally,
somebody like Van Gogh

01:16:30.910 --> 01:16:32.418
cutting off his own ear, you know?

01:16:32.418 --> 01:16:36.490
Like you have this, there's
this just cultural entity,

01:16:36.490 --> 01:16:37.323
which is

01:16:38.360 --> 01:16:41.490
you've got to drag yourself through

01:16:43.330 --> 01:16:46.570
a torturous experience
in order to come out

01:16:46.570 --> 01:16:48.280
on the other side in success.

01:16:48.280 --> 01:16:50.090
And it's like, well, first of all,

01:16:50.090 --> 01:16:53.940
the success on the other side
may be just as disorienting

01:16:53.940 --> 01:16:55.660
as the failure and the struggle.

01:16:55.660 --> 01:16:56.790
You are who you are

01:16:56.790 --> 01:16:59.455
and your baseline happiness
is kind of pre tuned.

01:16:59.455 --> 01:17:01.093
You know what I'm saying?

01:17:02.900 --> 01:17:05.970
So it's very difficult for me

01:17:05.970 --> 01:17:09.220
'cause I always have thought that,

01:17:09.220 --> 01:17:11.160
yeah, this is supposed to be this like,

01:17:11.160 --> 01:17:12.440
this is supposed to be a grind

01:17:12.440 --> 01:17:14.820
and you're really supposed
to just like throw yourself

01:17:14.820 --> 01:17:16.840
in this and be able to look
back and be like, aw, man

01:17:16.840 --> 01:17:18.780
we worked so hard on that
and then it became a thing,

01:17:18.780 --> 01:17:20.010
but it's just like, I don't know

01:17:20.010 --> 01:17:21.900
if it's gonna become a thing.

01:17:21.900 --> 01:17:26.510
Can I just learn to enjoy the steps?

01:17:26.510 --> 01:17:27.480
I mean, who know?

01:17:27.480 --> 01:17:30.440
If one of these things becomes
a reality, it'll be fun.

01:17:30.440 --> 01:17:31.580
It'll be awesome.

01:17:31.580 --> 01:17:34.200
But it'll also complicate our
lives in a certain way, right?

01:17:34.200 --> 01:17:38.280
Like, okay, we do 100
different things already.

01:17:38.280 --> 01:17:39.780
Let's throw 101 in there

01:17:39.780 --> 01:17:42.660
and this one will be
making a television show,

01:17:42.660 --> 01:17:43.493
making a movie?

01:17:43.493 --> 01:17:46.010
I'm not saying we won't
find a way to make it happen

01:17:46.010 --> 01:17:46.960
but you know what?

01:17:46.960 --> 01:17:47.970
It'll be stressful.

01:17:47.970 --> 01:17:52.050
It'll be a complicating factor in things.

01:17:52.050 --> 01:17:53.410
- Yeah.
- So it's not always

01:17:53.410 --> 01:17:55.710
like the success or something happening

01:17:55.710 --> 01:17:58.760
is gonna some incredible thing

01:17:58.760 --> 01:18:03.373
that is gonna change your life, you know?

01:18:04.890 --> 01:18:05.723
Yeah.

01:18:05.723 --> 01:18:07.540
So I think

01:18:09.350 --> 01:18:13.720
ultimately for me it's
just a constant battle

01:18:13.720 --> 01:18:17.090
to detach myself from the outcome

01:18:17.090 --> 01:18:19.310
and also when the outcome is failure,

01:18:19.310 --> 01:18:21.055
which is most often,

01:18:21.055 --> 01:18:22.510
especially with the
stuff that we're talking,

01:18:22.510 --> 01:18:25.320
it's in fact only almost
exclusively failure and rejection

01:18:25.320 --> 01:18:27.583
with the ideas that we've
talked about before.

01:18:28.900 --> 01:18:32.870
Seeing that there's probably
ultimately more longterm value

01:18:32.870 --> 01:18:35.030
in those rejections and failures

01:18:35.030 --> 01:18:37.000
than there would be in
one of them working out.

01:18:37.000 --> 01:18:41.010
- And I think my chief
struggle is something that

01:18:41.010 --> 01:18:45.410
will make more sense in light
of our Enneagram conversations

01:18:45.410 --> 01:18:46.940
over the next couple of weeks

01:18:46.940 --> 01:18:51.940
because my struggle is when
things are so speculative

01:18:52.370 --> 01:18:56.345
and they're not actually
like nuts and bolts of doing

01:18:56.345 --> 01:19:00.290
and like perfecting,

01:19:00.290 --> 01:19:02.610
like execute and then perfecting,

01:19:02.610 --> 01:19:04.730
like the next parts of
the creative process.

01:19:04.730 --> 01:19:07.200
Like if a project never
gets off the ground

01:19:07.200 --> 01:19:10.633
and that keeps happening and
happening, I get very dejected.

01:19:13.120 --> 01:19:15.310
I feel like I'm sitting
on my hands, you know?

01:19:15.310 --> 01:19:17.630
Because I'm not the writer.

01:19:17.630 --> 01:19:21.130
And you know, I'm not as much of the idea,

01:19:21.130 --> 01:19:22.830
from nothing comes an idea.

01:19:22.830 --> 01:19:24.920
It's more of like, yes,

01:19:24.920 --> 01:19:28.522
I get on board with those things

01:19:28.522 --> 01:19:30.690
and then let's go for it.

01:19:30.690 --> 01:19:31.540
- Right.
- You know?

01:19:31.540 --> 01:19:35.300
So there's a different
type of struggle for me

01:19:37.040 --> 01:19:40.640
in this kind of like, to stay engaged

01:19:40.640 --> 01:19:44.890
when it's, you know, our hands
have been tied with COVID

01:19:44.890 --> 01:19:48.150
but also so much is speculative.

01:19:48.150 --> 01:19:49.970
And now we're, you know,

01:19:49.970 --> 01:19:52.480
we've actually got people
whose full-time job

01:19:52.480 --> 01:19:54.450
as a development person.

01:19:54.450 --> 01:19:57.230
I don't think I could ever
be a development person

01:19:57.230 --> 01:19:59.640
because now that we're working closely

01:19:59.640 --> 01:20:02.840
with Mallory and Taylor

01:20:02.840 --> 01:20:06.700
who are like working for us
full-time to take our ideas,

01:20:06.700 --> 01:20:08.507
develop other ideas, bring projects in

01:20:08.507 --> 01:20:11.290
and like get traditional
things off the ground.

01:20:11.290 --> 01:20:16.290
It's like, it's very hard
for me to remain engaged

01:20:16.490 --> 01:20:18.550
because it's just so speculative.

01:20:18.550 --> 01:20:21.980
I can just be like this
is never gonna happen.

01:20:21.980 --> 01:20:25.159
I'm never gonna be able to
engage what I feel like my gifts

01:20:25.159 --> 01:20:27.750
and my passions are because we're just

01:20:30.760 --> 01:20:33.300
meandering around a starting line

01:20:33.300 --> 01:20:36.960
and then no one may ever
fire the gun to run.

01:20:36.960 --> 01:20:37.793
You know?

01:20:37.793 --> 01:20:39.900
It's like call me when
it's time to run, you know?

01:20:39.900 --> 01:20:41.103
And so it's been,

01:20:42.639 --> 01:20:47.420
that's what's been difficult
for me and it's not even,

01:20:47.420 --> 01:20:50.640
so then I start to
anticipate the rejection

01:20:50.640 --> 01:20:54.220
and just disengage, I
think to protect myself

01:20:54.220 --> 01:20:57.380
from the dejection of

01:20:58.510 --> 01:21:01.020
never getting to the part

01:21:01.020 --> 01:21:05.067
that's where I feel like

01:21:05.067 --> 01:21:08.260
I thrive and I can find my flow.

